### SKILL.md  [instructions]
- description: "Convert strength-training audio recordings or transcripts into clean WorkoutSummary notes for future reference, with exercise-name standardization, duration calculation, sets/reps counting, multilingual support, and optional speech-to-text backends."
- Use this skill to turn a strength-training recording or transcript into a compact `WorkoutSummary <date>` note that is useful for future reference.
- Load `references/summary-workflow.md` when you need implementation details, suggested commands, output shape, dataset matching guidance, or audio-format caveats.
- ## Quick Model
- 1. Accept a local strength-training recording or transcript file, usually `.m4a`, `.mp3`, `.wav`, `.caf`, `.md`, `.txt`, `.srt`, `.vtt`, or `.json`.
- 2. Pick a transcription backend that is already available, or guide setup if none is ready.
- 3. Preserve transcript timestamps when available.
- 4. Produce a concise workout summary in chat or stdout.
- 5. Do not upload to hosted storage, create remote sessions, or store vector indexes unless the user explicitly asks.
- ## Workflow
- 1. Confirm the source file exists; inspect size/container for audio or structure for transcripts.
- 2. If the audio is not ideal, convert, compress, or split it before transcription.
- 3. Choose the best ready transcription backend:
-    - local Whisper CLI when installed and privacy/offline use matters
-    - Groq Whisper when `GROQ_API_KEY` is configured
-    - OpenAI or another compatible transcription API when the user provides credentials
-    - an existing transcript file if audio transcription is unavailable
- 4. If no backend is ready, explain the options and ask the user which one to set up.
- 5. Transcribe with timestamps when possible.
- 6. Summarize the session into:
-    - title: `WorkoutSummary <date>`
-    - total workout length
-    - number of completed exercises
-    - exercise name
-    - duration, not raw time range
-    - groups/sets completed
-    - reps, duration, or holds completed
-    - trainer cues only when they add something beyond the reference exercise instructions
-    - homework or next-session notes
- 7. Print the summary. Save transcript or summary only when asked.
- ## Audio Prep
- Use local preprocessing when the input is too large, has an awkward container, or fails transcription:
- - Convert CAF/LPCM or unusual containers to WAV or low-bitrate `.m4a`.
- - Compress long recordings to mono, 16 kHz, around 24 kbps when quality is sufficient for speech.
- - Split recordings above the chosen backend's limit into ordered chunks, commonly 15-minute parts.
- - When splitting, preserve part order and add each part's start offset before summarizing timestamps.
- Useful commands:
- ```bash
- ffprobe -hide_banner "$AUDIO"
- ffmpeg -i input.caf -ac 1 -ar 16000 output.wav
- ffmpeg -i input.m4a -ac 1 -ar 16000 -b:a 24k output.m4a
- ffmpeg -i input.m4a -f segment -segment_time 900 -c copy part_%03d.m4a
- ## API Key
- Do not embed real API keys in this skill or any publishable package. Read service keys from environment variables such as `GROQ_API_KEY` or provider-specific equivalents, a local secret manager, or an OpenClaw secret mechanism supplied at runtime.
- ## Backend Options
- Prefer a backend that is ready on the user's machine. Do not force Groq if its key/session is not set up.
- - Local Whisper: no API key; good default for privacy and offline use if the `whisper` CLI is installed.
- - Groq Whisper: fast cloud transcription when `GROQ_API_KEY` is configured.
- - OpenAI or compatible APIs: use when the user already has that provider configured.
- - Existing transcript: summarize directly if the user already has `.txt`, `.srt`, `.vtt`, `.json`, or Markdown transcript output.
- ## Exercise Dataset Matching
- When possible, standardize exercise names against `hasaneyldrm/exercises-dataset`:
- - Repository: `https://github.com/hasaneyldrm/exercises-dataset`
- - Primary file: `data/exercises.json`
- - Useful fields: `name`, `category`, `body_part`, `equipment`, `target`, `muscle_group`, `secondary_muscles`, `instructions.<lang>`, and `instruction_steps.<lang>`
- - Supported instruction languages include English, Spanish, Italian, Turkish, Russian, Chinese, Hindi, Polish, and Korean.
- Dataset workflow rules:
- - Treat the dataset as an external lookup database, not prompt content.
- - Do not paste the full `exercises.json` into the conversation.
- - Load or cache the JSON locally, then build a lightweight searchable index.
- - Extract candidate exercise names from the transcript first.
- - Retrieve only the top matching dataset records for those candidates.
- - Pass only compact matched metadata and relevant instruction snippets into final summarization.
- - Use code or shell tools for fuzzy matching when available; use the LLM for judgment after narrowing candidates.
- - Prefer `scripts/match_exercises.py` for the first-pass fuzzy match when this skill package is available.
- Example:
- python scripts/match_exercises.py --lang zh --top-k 3 "深蹲" "dumbbell lateral raise"
- python scripts/match_exercises.py --candidates-file candidates.txt --lang en --pretty
- Include equipment or variant words in candidates when the transcript provides them, for example `哑铃侧平举` instead of only `侧平举`; generic names intentionally return multiple plausible variants for later disambiguation.
- Matching rules:
- - Detect the dominant transcript language and preserve the user's requested output language when known.
- - For Chinese or mixed Chinese/English sessions, match both translated exercise meaning and any English exercise names the trainer uses.
- - Normalize spoken names and dataset names by lowercasing where applicable, removing punctuation, and collapsing equipment/side variants.
- - Use fuzzy matching plus transcript context such as equipment, target body part, and trainer wording.
- - Use the dataset `name` as the canonical exercise name when confidence is high; include a translated label only if it helps the user.
- - If confidence is low, keep the trainer/spoken name and mark it as approximate instead of forcing a dataset match.
- - Do not write `dataset match` in the final summary; use the matched name directly as the exercise name.
- - Compare trainer cues against the dataset instructions in the transcript language when available, falling back to English.
- - Only include highlights that are specific, corrective, repeated, or materially different from the dataset's standard instructions.
- - Do not include generic cues already covered by the dataset unless the trainer personalized them for the user.
- ## Output Format
- Prefer this compact format:
- WorkoutSummary <date>
- Total length: 45:20
- Exercises completed: 6
- 1. Canonical exercise name, ~2 min, 2 groups * 10 reps/side
- - Direct note or cue; no `trainer-specific cue` label.
- Use duration on the exercise line, not raw time ranges. For split/non-contiguous groups, show total duration such as `~4 min total`. Use `scripts/calc_duration.py` when helpful. Use `about`, `roughly`, or `~` when the transcript is ambiguous. Count groups/sets and reps from what was actually completed, not just planned. Never invent sets, reps, weights, pain details, or medical conclusions.
- ## Important Caveats
- - Groq Whisper is an option, not a requirement.
- - Keep API keys out of output and committed files.
- - Cloud providers may have per-file limits; split long recordings when needed.
- - For Chinese/English mixed sessions, preserve the trainer's intent rather than translating literally when naming exercises or cues.
